  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "44116300",
      "title": "How To Connect Angular Apps To A Backend",
      "lectureOrder": 220,
      "videoOrder": 211,
      "sourceHash": "be2bec7a66d8a077e825b3feb0c8df9ac7a3b3576969b73d5b3bdb935db2da59",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 0,
          "end": 1.38,
          "en": "[Maximilian Schwarzmüller] Now when building",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 1.38,
          "end": 3.27,
          "en": "an Angular application",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 3.27,
          "end": 7.71,
          "en": "that should interact with data that's stored in files",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 7.71,
          "end": 11.67,
          "en": "or in a database, it's important to understand",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 11.67,
          "end": 15.54,
          "en": "that you don't directly talk to a database",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 15.54,
          "end": 18.063,
          "en": "from inside your Angular application.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 19.11,
          "end": 22.62,
          "en": "Instead, your Angular code in the end",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 22.62,
          "end": 26.58,
          "en": "runs in the browser of your website visitors",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 26.58,
          "end": 29.34,
          "en": "and therefore putting code in there that would connect",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 29.34,
          "end": 33.15,
          "en": "to a database and therefore contain database credentials,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 33.15,
          "end": 36.54,
          "en": "would be insecure, and there also very likely",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 36.54,
          "end": 38.91,
          "en": "would be performance issues and so on.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 38.91,
          "end": 41.79,
          "en": "So you don't directly talk to a database",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 41.79,
          "end": 44.04,
          "en": "from inside your Angular app.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 44.04,
          "end": 48.78,
          "en": "Instead, the idea is that you have some kind of web API,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 48.78,
          "end": 53.73,
          "en": "either one you built on your own or some third party API",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 53.73,
          "end": 56.25,
          "en": "you might want to use in your project,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 56.25,
          "end": 60.09,
          "en": "to which you then talk from inside your Angular app.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 60.09,
          "end": 63.66,
          "en": "So, which you then do connect to your Angular app.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 63.66,
          "end": 65.4,
          "en": "And therefore the main question is",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 65.4,
          "end": 69.36,
          "en": "how you connect an Angular app to a backend.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 69.36,
          "end": 72.9,
          "en": "So to such an API, for example.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 72.9,
          "end": 75.96000000000001,
          "en": "So how do you connect an Angular app",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 75.96000000000001,
          "end": 78.6,
          "en": "to a backend API?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 78.6,
          "end": 81.84,
          "en": "Well, it of course starts with that Angular app,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 81.84,
          "end": 85.68,
          "en": "the so-called front end or client-side application",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 85.68,
          "end": 89.673,
          "en": "that's running in the browsers of your website visitors.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 90.69,
          "end": 94.86,
          "en": "And you then have such a backend API as I mentioned before,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 94.86,
          "end": 97.5,
          "en": "the so-called server-side, which can be owned",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 97.5,
          "end": 100.56,
          "en": "and operated by you, but which could also be some API",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 100.56,
          "end": 102.81,
          "en": "provided by someone else.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 102.81,
          "end": 106.44,
          "en": "And what's important here is that the website visitor",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 106.44,
          "end": 109.83,
          "en": "won't have direct access to that API.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 109.83,
          "end": 113.1,
          "en": "It's running on some separate server and the code base there",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 113.1,
          "end": 117.42,
          "en": "and the data there is isolated from the outside world,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 117.42,
          "end": 122.42,
          "en": "except for some routes, some explicitly provided URLs",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 123.54,
          "end": 126.09,
          "en": "to which the front end application,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 126.09,
          "end": 130.02,
          "en": "the Angular application can send requests.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 130.02,
          "end": 132.63,
          "en": "And that is indeed what it will do.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 132.63,
          "end": 137.37,
          "en": "That is how you connect an Angular application to a backend.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 137.37,
          "end": 142.32,
          "en": "You send HTTP requests from inside the Angular app,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 142.32,
          "end": 146.25,
          "en": "so from inside your Angular code to that backend API",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 146.25,
          "end": 148.77,
          "en": "for example, to fetch some data,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 148.77,
          "end": 151.29,
          "en": "but also, for example, to send some data",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 151.29,
          "end": 153.45,
          "en": "to the backend and store it there.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 153.45,
          "end": 158.13,
          "en": "Both is possible and both will be covered in this section.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 158.13,
          "end": 163.13,
          "en": "Well, and that backend API then responds with a response,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 163.65,
          "end": 166.41,
          "en": "which for example includes the requested data,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 166.41,
          "end": 170.76,
          "en": "which can then be used from inside your Angular code again.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 170.76,
          "end": 174.36,
          "en": "So, which can then be used in the Angular application",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 174.36,
          "end": 176.313,
          "en": "to for example, update the UI.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 177.24,
          "end": 180.78,
          "en": "And that's what I want to do here, this application now.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 180.78,
          "end": 183.03,
          "en": "I want to send a request to the backend",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 183.03,
          "end": 187.77,
          "en": "that gives me some data about places we might want to visit,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 187.77,
          "end": 189.66,
          "en": "and we therefore can pick here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 189.66,
          "end": 194.013,
          "en": "that I can output in this available places box here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 195.15,
          "end": 198.96,
          "en": "And thankfully that backend API in the backend folder,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 198.96,
          "end": 203.96,
          "en": "has a route for that because it does accept get requests",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 205.47,
          "end": 210.423,
          "en": "sent to the backend domain slash places,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 211.95,
          "end": 214.8,
          "en": "and then there it will fetch the data from the database,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 214.8,
          "end": 217.32,
          "en": "or in this case, from this JSON file,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 217.32,
          "end": 221.463,
          "en": "and send back a response that contains those places.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 222.63,
          "end": 226.65,
          "en": "So our goal now is to send such HTTP request",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 226.65,
          "end": 231.65,
          "en": "from the Angular app to the backend by using this route,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 231.75,
          "end": 235.59300000000002,
          "en": "by sending a get request to this backend path.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 237.39,
          "end": 240.243,
          "en": "And that's what we'll do now in the next steps.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}
